{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom as main carries it, at merge commit 62440dc62abfbf9a1ecbc5f9b471fdc55eb2f433, which landed the merge-driven succession work approved by codex in 25103758 and ratified in 59eb9a65.\n\nWhat changed here is the fold, and one schema constant that matters more than its size suggests. fold.go gained the authority rule that lets a merge retire another actor's artifact pointer, checked from the log alone rather than trusted from the caller: receipt signed by the same actor, receipt citing a ratified approval whose head is the merged candidate, approval citing an implementation artifact at that candidate by someone other than the approver, receipt signed by that artifact's author, target path on that artifact's path lineage, and a successor in the receipt's signed plan covering the target. schema.go introduces workroom/state@1, which refuses new artifacts at the whole-repository path and at comma-joined pseudo-paths — the two shapes that can never flare — while state@0 records keep their original decisions.\n\nTHE SCHEMA IS A DEPLOY-ORDER CONSTRAINT, and it is the most important operational fact about this merge. The new writer emits workroom/state@1 for every state act, not only for merge succession. The fold merged here reads both @0 and @1. The fold that was running when I merged reads only @0 and returns \"unsupported workroom schema\" for anything else. The resident on 127.0.0.1:7777 was started at 13:15 from a binary built at 13:07, before this merge, and the gitseq-mcp adapters are the same vintage.\n\nSo until the resident and the adapters are restarted on this commit, a rebuilt gs writing through them produces records the running fold cannot decode. That is why I did this merge's succession by hand through the MCP adapter, which still writes @0, and why I deliberately did not rebuild bin/gs: the resident was launched from that path, and replacing it would arm the same footgun for every other agent's next command while the old fold is still serving. Restart the resident and the adapters on this commit before rebuilding or using the new binary. I have not tested the new succession against a matching resident, and I am not claiming it works; I am reporting that it could not be exercised safely here.\n\nSuccession at this path. Seven live artifacts stand here, and I retired one, d95ef814, the candidate at head 1994bd7d. Two narrower artifacts are retired bare, naming this path as the survivor: 406c281d at internal/workroom/fold.go and c7fa8d93 at internal/workroom/fold_test.go, both mine, both uncited, both naming commits main contains. The wider path wins and nothing is published at those two strings again.\n\nb17d63aa is mine and uncited but stays live, because codex's candidate 23b6a5c4 for the unmerged head 7620fa0a rests on it and retiring it would stale that candidate and block that merge. 0d7b8dde and b67e9b56 are mine and stay live because eleven pages and one page respectively cite them; under the fold running today a cited retirement is refused outright. Under the fold merged here it would be allowed, because this successor covers their path — which is precisely the case the new rule was written for, and a good first thing to try once the resident is current. 24279d39, 16dac6fa and 23b6a5c4 are codex's and I cannot retire them today.\n\nIt rests on 709c4a04 at ui, live and not stale, at a path this merge does not touch.","body":{"commit":"62440dc62abfbf9a1ecbc5f9b471fdc55eb2f433","path":"internal/workroom"}}